WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.085 --> 00:00:04.562
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.562 --> 00:00:09.089
Last weekend,
I was riding in an Uber Express Pool.

00:00:09.089 --> 00:00:13.910
My fellow female passenger was angry she
was dropped so far from her destination.

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:17.080
So she tried to punch the driver and
steal his phone.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:21.890
The driver got out of the car,
pushing her away, defending himself.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:25.800
The cop sped up, pulling the two apart,
assuming it was the driver's fault.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:31.840
As he was being interrogated by
the police, he kept begging for his phone.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.140
I need to turn off my Uber app, he pled.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.063
If I don't pick up my next client,
they will charge me for

00:00:41.063 --> 00:00:44.785
the trip and
deactivate me from the platform.

00:00:44.785 --> 00:00:49.519
This 60-something Korean man
was more afraid of the wrath of

00:00:49.519 --> 00:00:53.880
Uber than the wrath of the police,
and rightly so.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.540
Uber could have unilaterally cut
off his livelihood with no hearing,

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.040
no notice, and no appeal.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:04.150
This driver is an independent contractor.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:07.420
Which in theory means he should
have complete independence

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.470
over setting his own rates,
working conditions, and contract terms.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:16.920
However, gate platforms like
Uber use changing incentives and

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.900
the threat of deactivation to
exert unprecedented control

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.219
over their allegedly
independent work force.

00:01:24.905 --> 00:01:28.770
These statements may lead you to
believe that I'm antibusiness.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:31.480
But actually I have worked at Uber.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.300
When I joined the company,
running their East Africa operations,

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.030
I was excited about creating thousands
of economic opportunities for

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:45.030
people in Kenya, Tanzania, and Uganda
where unemployment rates are really high.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:48.640
And I was excited about improving
mobility in today's hungry for

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.610
convenient, affordable transportation.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.990
But that excitement quickly
gave way to disillusionment.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:00.210
Every two weeks, I hosted focus
groups with around 20 drivers.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:04.720
They told me, Alyssa,
we're making no money on this platform.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.110
We work for 100 hours every week.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.606
And we only take home $20.

00:02:09.606 --> 00:02:13.210
When I ran the historical data,
their stories checked out.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:19.080
When Uber first launched in East Africa,
they offered very generous rates and

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.650
bonuses to make the economic
opportunity a no-brainer.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:27.570
Drivers, with the encouragement of Uber,
took out multi-year loans

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:31.060
to finance the relatively new vehicles
required to join the platform.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.800
One year after launch, and
two weeks after I joined the company,

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:40.769
we slashed rates by 35%,
shifting all of that onto drivers.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.160
It would not be the last bait and
switch the company would pull.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.120
When I raised concerns about
driver earnings to management,

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.730
their message to me was clear.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.740
Gig workers are an expendable resource for
growth.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:01.260
Because of the company's labor practices,
I quit after a short six months,

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.430
to ironically,
become an independent worker myself.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:10.288
As an independent worker, I faced many
challenges, like clients ghosting when it

00:03:10.288 --> 00:03:15.263
came time to make their final payment, and
going 18 months without health insurance.

00:03:15.263 --> 00:03:19.412
In Kenya, 80% of adults are self-employed.

00:03:19.412 --> 00:03:23.933
This means that you have no guaranteed
salary, no retirement contributions,

00:03:23.933 --> 00:03:27.104
no paid leave,
no employer-sponsored benefits, and

00:03:27.104 --> 00:03:29.269
you have to pay all your own expenses.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:33.547
The issue of the lack of protections for

00:03:33.547 --> 00:03:37.976
contract workers may seem like
a distant emerging market problem.

00:03:37.976 --> 00:03:42.609
But the US economy is evolving
to look like Kenya's.

00:03:42.609 --> 00:03:45.651
Since 2010,
the IRS has reported a 6% year on

00:03:45.651 --> 00:03:50.271
year increase in the number of independent
relative to full-time workers.

00:03:50.271 --> 00:03:53.629
By 2020,
half of Americans will be self-employed.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.887
Full time W2 workers in this country had
several protections thanks to the labor

00:03:59.887 --> 00:04:03.898
movement, which started around
the time of the Industrial Revolution.

00:04:03.898 --> 00:04:06.938
Factory owners treated their
workers as expendable,

00:04:06.938 --> 00:04:10.580
forcing them to work 100 hour
weeks just to maximize profits.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:16.130
Workers organized into unions and
rioted, resulting in

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:20.540
the 1938 Fair Labor Standards Act,
which instituted the 40-hour work week,

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:24.530
banned child labor and set standards
around minimum wage and overtime pay.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:31.070
None of these protections apply to
contractors, which threatens to

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:34.620
increase the unprecedented levels of
inequality we see in this country.

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:40.510
Corporations are systemically
contorting full-time positions

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:44.940
into contract positions to
minimize their fixed labor costs.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:49.470
Oftentimes, contractors will work for
the same single company for years.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:54.055
By changing nothing more than that
individual's formal employment status,

00:04:54.055 --> 00:04:58.980
companies are side-stepping the hard-won
worker protections of the 20th century and

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.380
shifting risk from investors to workers.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:07.110
Why do I care so
much about this misclassification issue?

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:12.868
Well, my dad was classified as
an independent contractor for

00:05:12.868 --> 00:05:15.911
the 25 years he worked at his company.

00:05:15.911 --> 00:05:20.240
He received less than $20,000 and
received no benefits.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.966
If my mom, a public school teacher,
did not have generous benefits,

00:05:25.966 --> 00:05:28.395
I have no idea how he
would have retired or

00:05:28.395 --> 00:05:31.904
my family would have accessed
quality health insurance.

00:05:35.323 --> 00:05:40.040
Some of the worst offenders of contract
worker abuse are in our own backyard.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:45.877
I learned five minutes before this
session started that Stanford is

00:05:45.877 --> 00:05:50.862
increasingly shifting to contractors
rather than full time employees to reduce

00:05:50.862 --> 00:05:52.560
the benefit obligations.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.540
Such as giving their employees free
college tuition for their children.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:01.930
The communications coaches who
helped us with these presentations

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:04.620
are in short term rather
than full time contracts.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:10.299
Google has more contractors on its
payroll than full time employees.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:17.870
How many of you have ordered something
on Amazon Prime in the last month?

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:19.128
All of us, same.

00:06:19.128 --> 00:06:24.307
[LAUGH] Well, chances are that
that package was delivered to you

00:06:24.307 --> 00:06:29.289
by an Amazon flex contract employee,
who, post expenses,

00:06:29.289 --> 00:06:36.350
can make as little as $5 an hour, under
half of California's $11 minimum wage.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:42.320
Contract workers in Arizona moderate
explicit content on the Facebook platform.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.840
They spend hours every day watching
horrific videos of murder and

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:46.880
sexual exploitation.

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.780
Several suffer from PTSD.

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:51.850
Which Facebook, of course,

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:56.720
provides no support for, because they're
just contractors, not employees.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:04.837
These contractors make $15 an hour,
or a mere $28,000 a year.

00:07:06.838 --> 00:07:10.116
Silicon Valley, fed by Stanford talent,

00:07:10.116 --> 00:07:14.950
has profited from driving down
the wages of low scale workers.

00:07:16.312 --> 00:07:18.210
We've moved backwards as as a society,

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:21.300
back to where we were at the start
of the Industrial Revolution.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.630
Back to the age of the 100 hour work
week and no effective minimum wage.

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:29.371
Back to the age where workers' rights
are dependent on the benevolence of

00:07:29.371 --> 00:07:30.288
the management.

00:07:30.288 --> 00:07:35.869
Dependent on the whims of the people in
this room, rather than protected by law.

00:07:37.553 --> 00:07:42.070
The good news is that gig workers
are organizing to demand their rights.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:45.930
In the past few weeks,
Instacart shoppers took to Reddit and

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:50.740
Facebook forums to oppose
Instacart's policy of deducting tips

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:52.830
from their minimum guaranteed base pay.

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:55.910
This time, the workers won.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:02.348
Instacart agreed to change its policy and
pay out tips separately from base pay,

00:08:02.348 --> 00:08:06.000
and they instituted new
minimum payments for delivery.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.402
David won against Goliath.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.020
I'd like to leave you with this.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:17.630
By being at the GSB,
we are in a place of incredible privilege.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:23.582
Our starting salary coming out of this
school will be $110,000 higher than, or

00:08:23.582 --> 00:08:28.399
2.8x the median household
income in the United States.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:33.860
Your employer will almost certainly give
you health insurance, and paid leave and

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:37.860
retirement contribution on
top of dinners at Nobu,

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:40.411
free trips to Cancun and
soul cycle classes.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:46.580
I say this not to guilt you, but
to implore you to be a voice for

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:48.900
those who do not have
the same level of privilege.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.439
You may be wondering what you can do.

00:08:52.439 --> 00:08:54.712
Well, you can start by tipping.

00:08:54.712 --> 00:08:56.655
Tip generously, and tip with cash or

00:08:56.655 --> 00:09:00.825
another former payment where you know
the funds will directly reach workers.

00:09:00.825 --> 00:09:05.388
Beyond tipping,
you can email HR if you witness contract

00:09:05.388 --> 00:09:09.389
worker mistreatment
within your organization.

00:09:09.389 --> 00:09:13.930
If you work in big tech,
chances are that mistreatment exists.

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:16.900
If you see contract workers
working crazy hours, or

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:20.165
earning wages that post expenses
are below minimum wage, or

00:09:20.165 --> 00:09:24.740
see workers doing the work of a full time
employee, so they deserve full benefits.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:29.760
And if your management is
unwilling to listen like mine was,

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.190
you can have the courage to go
to the press, even anonymously.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:40.428
Publications like the New York Times,
Recode, and Bloomberg have exposed stories

00:09:40.428 --> 00:09:45.197
that have shamed companies like Facebook
and Instacart into making changes.

00:09:47.845 --> 00:09:51.724
The disappearance of quality
employment opportunities threatens to

00:09:51.724 --> 00:09:56.193
exacerbate the unprecedented levels of
inequality that we see in our country and

00:09:56.193 --> 00:09:57.585
our Bay Area community.

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:04.340
Shirking your ethical responsibilities
to workers may seem like smart business.

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:08.790
But it stunts the economic mobility
of your workers and their children.

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:16.734
Don't be a part of the problem, be a part
of solution towards just employment.

00:10:16.734 --> 00:10:17.606
Thank you.

00:10:17.606 --> 00:10:22.176
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:22.176 --> 00:10:25.080
[MUSIC]

